import type { Article } from "../articles";

export const microdoseSectionsA: Article["sections"] = [
  {
    heading: "What is GLP-1 microdosing?",
    body: `<p>GLP-1 microdosing means taking a GLP-1 medicine such as semaglutide (Ozempic, Wegovy) or tirzepatide (Mounjaro, Zepbound) at a lower dose, or less often, than the FDA-approved label describes. There is no medical or regulatory definition of the term. In May 2026, endocrinologist Jody Dushay wrote in <a href="https://www.statnews.com/2026/05/29/glp-1-microdose-popular-but-unsupported-by-evidence/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">STAT</a> that "there isn't even a single definition of microdosing for weight loss or any other condition."</p>
<p>Because nobody owns the word, people use it for at least four different things. Reporting from <a href="https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2537964181" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Wall Street Journal (May 24, 2025, via syndicated copy)</a> and the clinicians quoted throughout this guide describe:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Doses below the labeled starting dose</strong>, for example less than 0.25 mg of semaglutide or less than 2.5 mg of tirzepatide a week.</li>
<li><strong>Staying on a starter dose for the long term</strong>, even though labels describe starter doses as the first step of dose escalation.</li>
<li><strong>Stretching the interval</strong> between injections, so doses are taken less often than the label describes.</li>
<li><strong>Drawing partial doses</strong> from a vial or pen that was made to deliver a fixed dose.</li>
</ul>
<p>Companies that sell low-dose programs add their own definitions. AgelessRx, which markets low-dose semaglutide for longevity, <a href="https://agelessrx.com/glp1-microdosing-trial/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">describes microdosing as 0.1 to 0.5 mg of semaglutide weekly</a>. That is a sales definition, not a clinical one, and we include it only so you can recognize it. The Obesity Medicine Association's Access and Policy Working Group, writing in <a href="https://www.sochob.cl/web1/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Microdosing-of-obesity-medications-A-Perspective-Statement-from-the-Access-and-Policy-Working-Group.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Obesity Pillars (2026)</a>, describes microdosing as fractional doses of the FDA-approved amount. It notes that these doses are "not available in FDA-approved pens," so they usually depend on compounded products.</p>
<p>Clinicians draw one more line that matters. <a href="https://www.cedars-sinai.org/stories-and-insights/expert-advice/can-microdosing-glp-1s-promote-health-and-weight-loss" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cedars-Sinai</a> separates supervised use of lower doses of FDA-approved GLP-1s "as part of a titration strategy" from "the microdosing strategies you see trending online." A doctor keeping you on a lower labeled dose for side effects is not the same as a social media dosing plan.</p>
<h3>The evidence ladder: what supports each microdosing claim (as of November 2026)</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Claim you may see</th><th>Best evidence that exists</th><th>Evidence type</th><th>Our verdict</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>"Microdosing causes meaningful weight loss"</td><td>No trial of sub-label or "microdose" regimens has reported results. Lower doses in dose-ranging trials produced less weight loss than higher doses (<a href="https://www.ajmc.com/view/semaglutide-produces-significant-sustained-weight-loss-for-adults-with-obesity-in-phase-2-trial" target="_blank" rel="noopener">semaglutide phase 2</a>; <a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2206038" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SURMOUNT-1</a>)</td><td>RCTs of labeled or trial doses only; none of microdosing as marketed</td><td>Not shown</td></tr>
<tr><td>"A lower dose can keep weight off"</td><td>SURMOUNT-MAINTAIN: stepping down to labeled Zepbound 5 mg kept more weight off than placebo but less than staying on 10 or 15 mg (<a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(26)00656-2/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lancet 2026</a>)</td><td>RCT (labeled dose, after full titration)</td><td>Supported for labeled 5 mg tirzepatide only</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Microdosing means fewer side effects"</td><td>Nausea and side-effect dropouts were lower at lower labeled doses in some trials (<a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/spl/data/dcabc03d-6a72-467a-8043-9eb12ce9c856/dcabc03d-6a72-467a-8043-9eb12ce9c856.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zepbound label</a>)</td><td>RCT data on labeled doses</td><td>Partly plausible, untested for microdoses</td></tr>
<tr><td>"People who microdose feel better on it"</td><td>Side-effect management was the top reason given by microdosers in the <a href="https://www.ispor.org/heor-resources/presentations-database/presentation-cti/ispor-2026/poster-session-4-3/emerging-patterns-of-glp-1-microdosing-in-a-large-real-world-population" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Evidation ISPOR 2026 survey</a></td><td>Self-reported survey</td><td>Reported, not measured</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Microdosing slows aging or helps you live longer"</td><td>One registered microdose trial with no posted results; one mouse study (<a href="https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/glp-1-weight-loss-medication-could-slow-aging-and-extend-lifespan/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scientific American</a>)</td><td>Animal data; no human results</td><td>No human evidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Microdosing lowers inflammation"</td><td>No human randomized trial of microdoses has reported results (<a href="https://www.sciencenews.org/article/glp1-medications-microdose-longevity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Science News</a>)</td><td>None</td><td>No evidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Microdosing protects your heart"</td><td>SELECT showed cardiovascular benefit at semaglutide 2.4 mg, not at low doses (<a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/215256s024lbl.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wegovy label</a>)</td><td>RCT at full dose only</td><td>Not shown for low doses</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Microdosing is safe because the dose is small"</td><td>FDA reports of dosing errors and overdoses with compounded semaglutide (<a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/fda-alerts-health-care-providers-compounders-and-patients-dosing-errors-associated-compounded" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA, July 2024</a>)</td><td>Regulator safety reports</td><td>Not established; new risks exist</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>What this means for you:</strong> when someone says "microdose," ask which of the four meanings they intend, and which product. A lower labeled dose prescribed by your clinician sits on the top rungs of that ladder. A self-chosen fraction of a compounded vial sits on the bottom.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Does GLP-1 microdosing work for weight loss?",
    body: `<p>No randomized trial has shown that microdosing a GLP-1 such as semaglutide or tirzepatide produces meaningful weight loss, because no trial of microdosing as it is marketed has reported results as of November 2026. What trials do show is that lower doses of semaglutide and tirzepatide produce less average weight loss than higher doses, and that a labeled lower maintenance dose of tirzepatide (5 mg) keeps more weight off than stopping.</p>
<p>The clinicians who treat obesity say the same thing in plain words. "We don't have any evidence that low-dose, intermittent use of GLP-1s provides meaningful or sustained weight loss," Amanda Velazquez, MD, director of Obesity Medicine at <a href="https://www.cedars-sinai.org/stories-and-insights/expert-advice/can-microdosing-glp-1s-promote-health-and-weight-loss" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cedars-Sinai</a>, said in 2026. In November 2025, <a href="https://www.statnews.com/2025/11/04/microdosing-glp-1-drugs-no-clinical-evidence-effective/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">STAT</a> reported that physicians and researchers see no robust clinical evidence that semaglutide or tirzepatide work at very small doses.</p>
<h3>What has been tested, and what has not</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tested:</strong> dose-ranging trials, where semaglutide and tirzepatide were given at several fixed doses after a planned dose escalation. Weight loss rose with dose (<a href="https://www.ajmc.com/view/semaglutide-produces-significant-sustained-weight-loss-for-adults-with-obesity-in-phase-2-trial" target="_blank" rel="noopener">semaglutide phase 2, 2018</a>; <a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2206038" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SURMOUNT-1, 2022</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Tested:</strong> stepping down to a lower labeled dose after weight loss, in SURMOUNT-MAINTAIN (<a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(26)00656-2/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lancet, May 2026</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Not tested with published results:</strong> doses below the labeled starting dose, stretched intervals between injections, partial doses drawn from pens or vials, and compounded products measured in "units."</li>
</ul>
<p>The gap matters because the tested lower doses are not that low. The smallest maintenance dose of Zepbound, 5 mg, still produced 15.0% average weight loss at 72 weeks in <a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2206038" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SURMOUNT-1</a> (treatment-regimen estimand, versus 3.1% on placebo). That is a full labeled dose given after titration, not a microdose. Sections 5 to 7 walk through the numbers.</p>
<p>Some people do report keeping weight off at low doses. The <a href="https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2537964181" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wall Street Journal</a> reported in 2025 that doctors see some "super responders" who keep weight off at low doses, but that this is unusual. Individual stories cannot tell you whether you would be one of them.</p>
<p><strong>The honest answer for you:</strong> if your goal is weight loss, the doses with proven results are the labeled ones. If a full dose is hard to tolerate, labels already allow slower escalation and lower maintenance doses, which is a conversation to have with your prescriber rather than a dose to choose on your own.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "How many people microdose GLP-1s, and why?",
    body: `<p>About 15% of people currently using an injectable GLP-1 such as semaglutide or tirzepatide say they have microdosed, according to the largest survey so far: 15.0% of 8,486 current injectable users in an Evidation analysis presented at <a href="https://www.ispor.org/heor-resources/presentations-database/presentation-cti/ispor-2026/poster-session-4-3/emerging-patterns-of-glp-1-microdosing-in-a-large-real-world-population" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISPOR 2026</a> (9.3% currently, 5.7% formerly). The top reasons were managing side effects, cost and maintaining weight.</p>
<p>Read that figure with its limits in mind. Evidation runs an online health community, and the data are self-reported answers to a cross-sectional 51-item survey, not prescription records. Respondents opted in, so they may not represent all GLP-1 users, and the survey's definition of microdosing may differ from your clinician's. The poster was a conference abstract, not a full peer-reviewed paper. With those caveats, the sample is large: <a href="https://www.ispor.org/heor-resources/presentations-database/presentation-cti/ispor-2026/poster-session-4-3/emerging-patterns-of-glp-1-microdosing-in-a-large-real-world-population" target="_blank" rel="noopener">79,602 people responded and 75,886 met analytic criteria</a> after quality checks.</p>
<h3>Microdosing in the Evidation survey (self-reported, online health community)</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Figure</th><th>Who was asked</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Ever microdosed</td><td>15.0% (9.3% current, 5.7% former)</td><td>8,486 current injectable GLP-1 users</td><td><a href="https://www.ispor.org/heor-resources/presentations-database/presentation-cti/ispor-2026/poster-session-4-3/emerging-patterns-of-glp-1-microdosing-in-a-large-real-world-population" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISPOR 2026</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Reason: managing side effects</td><td>40.8%</td><td>Microdosers</td><td><a href="https://www.ispor.org/heor-resources/presentations-database/presentation-cti/ispor-2026/poster-session-4-3/emerging-patterns-of-glp-1-microdosing-in-a-large-real-world-population" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISPOR 2026</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Reason: cost</td><td>31.9%</td><td>Microdosers</td><td><a href="https://www.ispor.org/heor-resources/presentations-database/presentation-cti/ispor-2026/poster-session-4-3/emerging-patterns-of-glp-1-microdosing-in-a-large-real-world-population" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISPOR 2026</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Reason: weight maintenance</td><td>29.6%</td><td>Microdosers</td><td><a href="https://www.ispor.org/heor-resources/presentations-database/presentation-cti/ispor-2026/poster-session-4-3/emerging-patterns-of-glp-1-microdosing-in-a-large-real-world-population" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISPOR 2026</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Expect to stop GLP-1 treatment within a year</td><td>51.0%</td><td>Current microdosers</td><td><a href="https://evidation.com/resources/ispor-2026-recap" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Evidation ISPOR recap</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Started on a standard regimen before switching</td><td>About half</td><td>Microdosers (preliminary data, Dec 2025)</td><td><a href="https://evidation.com/resources/real-world-glp-1-dosing-behaviors" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Evidation, Dec 16, 2025</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Used a compounded GLP-1</td><td>About half</td><td>Microdosers (preliminary data, Dec 2025)</td><td><a href="https://evidation.com/resources/real-world-glp-1-dosing-behaviors" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Evidation, Dec 16, 2025</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Interested in trying microdosing</td><td>Nearly one-third</td><td>People who had never taken a GLP-1</td><td><a href="https://businesswire.com/news/home/20251216728556/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Business Wire, Dec 16, 2025</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The never-users who were interested gave three reasons in the <a href="https://evidation.com/resources/real-world-glp-1-dosing-behaviors" target="_blank" rel="noopener">December 2025 release</a>: cost, concern about muscle loss, and wanting flexibility with their dose. The ISPOR authors describe microdosing as a patient-driven practice linked to perceived tolerability gains and a shorter expected time on treatment, per <a href="https://evidation.com/resources/ispor-2026-recap" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Evidation's recap</a>. "Perceived" is the right word: the survey recorded what people believed, not measured side effects or weight.</p>
<p>You may also see a much higher number. A 2025 poll by the practice-software company <a href="https://www.tebra.com/theintake/healthcare-reports/microdosing-glp-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tebra</a> reported that 36% of respondents were currently microdosing, but it was a small commercial online poll of 640 people and is not comparable with the Evidation data.</p>
<h3>How many people use GLP-1s at all</h3>
<p>For scale, <a href="https://news.gallup.com/poll/712157/glp-usage-reaches-new-high.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gallup reported on July 7, 2026</a> that 11% of US adults say they currently take a GLP-1 for weight loss, up from 3% in 2024 (n=5,065). Among those current users, 68% use brand-name drugs, 19% use a compounded or "custom-mixed" version and 12% are not sure which, per <a href="https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/glp-1s/glp-1-use-hits-record-high-in-us-as-obesity-rates-fall-6-things-to-know/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Becker's summary of the Gallup data</a>. In a <a href="https://www.kff.org/public-opinion/poll-1-in-8-adults-say-they-are-currently-taking-a-glp-1-drug-for-weight-loss-diabetes-or-another-condition-even-as-half-say-the-drugs-are-difficult-to-afford/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KFF poll published November 14, 2025</a>, 56% of GLP-1 users said the drugs were difficult to afford, and 13% said they had stopped because of side effects. KFF did not ask about lowering doses.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Where do people who microdose get their medication?",
    body: `<p>Most people who microdose a GLP-1 do not get the drug from their own regular clinician. In Evidation survey data reported by <a href="https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/patients-microdose-glp-1s-without-clinician-input-2026a10002ih" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Medscape on January 27, 2026</a>, only 39.8% of active microdosers got their semaglutide or tirzepatide from their current healthcare clinician, compared with 60.2% of standard-dose users who had never microdosed. Almost 24% of active microdosers used a telehealth service.</p>
<p>The same survey limits apply as in the previous section: these are self-reported answers from an online health community, not pharmacy records. The ISPOR 2026 abstract adds that current microdosers were more likely than other users to get medication from sources outside their own healthcare provider, <a href="https://www.ispor.org/heor-resources/presentations-database/presentation-cti/ispor-2026/poster-session-4-3/emerging-patterns-of-glp-1-microdosing-in-a-large-real-world-population" target="_blank" rel="noopener">such as telehealth companies or medspas</a>.</p>
<h3>Where microdosers get their drug and their information (Evidation data, self-reported)</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Active microdosers</th><th>Comparison group</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Got GLP-1 from current healthcare clinician</td><td>39.8%</td><td>60.2% of 5,611 standard-regimen users who never microdosed</td><td><a href="https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/patients-microdose-glp-1s-without-clinician-input-2026a10002ih" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Medscape, Jan 2026</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Got GLP-1 from a telehealth service</td><td>Almost 24%</td><td>Not reported in our source</td><td><a href="https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/patients-microdose-glp-1s-without-clinician-input-2026a10002ih" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Medscape, Jan 2026</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Decided to microdose on their own</td><td>32.6%</td><td>Among those who microdosed while on a standard regimen</td><td><a href="https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/patients-microdose-glp-1s-without-clinician-input-2026a10002ih" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Medscape, Jan 2026</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Most trusted information source: social media</td><td>26.8%</td><td>Online research 24.4%; healthcare clinician 20.3%</td><td><a href="https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/patients-microdose-glp-1s-without-clinician-input-2026a10002ih" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Medscape, Jan 2026</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Used a compounded GLP-1</td><td>About half of microdosers</td><td>19% of all current US users (Gallup, a different survey)</td><td><a href="https://evidation.com/resources/real-world-glp-1-dosing-behaviors" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Evidation, Dec 2025</a>; <a href="https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/glp-1s/glp-1-use-hits-record-high-in-us-as-obesity-rates-fall-6-things-to-know/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Becker's</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The pattern is consistent: microdosers lean more on telehealth, compounded products and social media, and less on their own doctor. Social media was the leading source of microdosing information across all participants in the <a href="https://www.ispor.org/heor-resources/presentations-database/presentation-cti/ispor-2026/poster-session-4-3/emerging-patterns-of-glp-1-microdosing-in-a-large-real-world-population" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISPOR analysis</a>. Many licensed telehealth prescribers follow labeled dosing; the concern is dose decisions made without anyone checking your medical history.</p>
<h3>Dose stretching to stay on brand-name drugs</h3>
<p>Some people take less simply because of cost. <a href="https://www.usnews.com/news/top-news/articles/2025-08-13/to-stay-on-weight-loss-drugs-us-patients-cut-doses-and-maybe-vacations" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reuters reported in August 2025</a> that, as insurers restricted coverage, US patients were "stretching doses" or cutting other spending to stay on Wegovy and Zepbound, and that some made vials last longer. If cost is your reason, there may be cheaper labeled options to raise with your prescriber; a later section of this guide works through the cost math.</p>
<p><strong>One firm line:</strong> get any GLP-1 only with a prescription from a licensed clinician and from a state-licensed pharmacy. Products sold as "research peptides" or "for research use only" are not made for people, and no dose of them can be called a microdose of anything, because you cannot know what is in the vial. Cedars-Sinai's advice applies to every source: "everyone should avoid self-directed or unsupervised use of GLP-1 medications, no matter the dose" (<a href="https://www.cedars-sinai.org/stories-and-insights/expert-advice/can-microdosing-glp-1s-promote-health-and-weight-loss" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cedars-Sinai, 2026</a>).</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "What do trials show about lower doses of semaglutide?",
    body: `<p>Trials show that semaglutide's weight loss rises with dose: in a 52-week phase 2 dose-ranging trial, the lowest daily dose produced 6.0% average weight loss and the highest produced 13.8%, versus 2.3% on placebo (<a href="https://www.ajmc.com/view/semaglutide-produces-significant-sustained-weight-loss-for-adults-with-obesity-in-phase-2-trial" target="_blank" rel="noopener">O'Neil et al., Lancet 2018, via AJMC</a>). No semaglutide trial in our sources has reported weight-loss results for doses below Wegovy's 0.25 mg weekly starting dose.</p>
<p>That phase 2 trial is the closest thing to a semaglutide "low dose" study, and it is often misread. It randomized <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327093887" target="_blank" rel="noopener">957 adults with obesity and without diabetes</a> to once-daily semaglutide at 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3 or 0.4 mg, liraglutide 3.0 mg, or placebo, with semaglutide escalated every 4 weeks to the target dose. Two details matter:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The doses were daily.</strong> Added up (our arithmetic from the <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327093887" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trial design</a>), they come to roughly 0.35 to 2.8 mg a week, so even the lowest arm received more than Wegovy's 0.25 mg weekly starting dose. That arithmetic is a rough comparison only: daily dosing produces a different drug-level pattern from weekly dosing, and the two are not proven equivalent.</li>
<li><strong>Lower doses did less.</strong> Lipids, blood pressure and HbA1c improved at 0.2 mg a day or more, per <a href="https://conscienhealth.org/2018/08/17/a-good-look-at-semaglutide-for-obesity/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ConscienHealth's review of the paper</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Semaglutide and liraglutide results by dose</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Drug and dose</th><th>Trial (population)</th><th>Duration</th><th>Estimand</th><th>Average weight change vs placebo</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Semaglutide 0.05 / 0.1 / 0.2 / 0.3 / 0.4 mg daily</td><td>Phase 2 dose-ranging, n=957 (obesity, no diabetes) (<a href="https://www.ajmc.com/view/semaglutide-produces-significant-sustained-weight-loss-for-adults-with-obesity-in-phase-2-trial" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AJMC</a>)</td><td>52 weeks</td><td>Not stated in our source</td><td>-6.0% / -8.6% / -11.6% / -11.2% / -13.8% vs -2.3%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Semaglutide 1.0 mg weekly</td><td>STEP 2, n=1,210 (type 2 diabetes) (<a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(21)00213-0/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lancet 2021</a>)</td><td>68 weeks</td><td>Primary analysis as published; label not in our source</td><td>-7.0% vs -3.4%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly</td><td>STEP 2 (same trial)</td><td>68 weeks</td><td>Same as above</td><td>-9.6% vs -3.4%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly</td><td>STEP 1, n=1,961 (no diabetes) (<a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2032183" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NEJM 2021</a>)</td><td>68 weeks</td><td>Treatment-policy</td><td>-14.9% vs -2.4% (no lower-dose arm)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Oral semaglutide 25 mg daily (Wegovy pill)</td><td>OASIS 4, n=307 (<a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMoa2500969" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NEJM 2025</a>)</td><td>64 weeks</td><td>Treatment-policy</td><td>-13.6% vs -2.2%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Liraglutide 1.8 mg vs 3.0 mg daily</td><td>SCALE Diabetes, n=846 (type 2 diabetes) (<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26284720/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JAMA 2015</a>)</td><td>56 weeks</td><td>Not stated in our source</td><td>-4.7% (1.8 mg) and -6.0% (3.0 mg) vs -2.0%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>STEP 2 is the only large semaglutide trial in our sources with a lower weekly dose side by side with 2.4 mg. It enrolled adults with type 2 diabetes, so its numbers should not be compared directly with STEP 1, which enrolled adults without diabetes. In STEP 2, <a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(21)00213-0/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">69% on 2.4 mg and 57% on 1.0 mg</a> lost at least 5% of their weight, versus 29% on placebo. The older drug liraglutide shows the same direction: in <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26284720/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SCALE Diabetes</a>, 54.3% on 3.0 mg and 40.4% on 1.8 mg reached at least 5% loss, versus 21.4% on placebo.</p>
<p>For the Wegovy pill, OASIS 4 tested only the 25 mg maintenance dose; no published OASIS 4 data show what happens if people stay on the 1.5 mg or 4 mg starter tablets (<a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMoa2500969" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NEJM 2025</a>). Novo materials cite 16.6% versus 2.7%, which appears to be the trial-product estimand.</p>
<h3>What the Wegovy label allows</h3>
<p>The Wegovy injection label lists two maintenance doses for adult weight reduction, <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/215256s024lbl.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2.4 mg (recommended) or 1.7 mg</a>, and says the maintenance dose can be lowered if 2.4 mg is not tolerated. No trial result for 1.7 mg appears in the sources we checked, so we cannot give you a number for it. Meanwhile the approved range is moving up, not down: FDA approved <a href="https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/fda-approves-novo-nordisks-new-wegovy-hd-injection-delivering-the-highest-weight-loss-to-date-for-a-wegovy-injection-adding-to-its-already-expansive-clinical-profile-302718982.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wegovy HD (7.2 mg weekly) on March 19, 2026</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> lower semaglutide doses still work, but they work less, and every tested "low" dose is at or above a labeled starting dose. If a smaller semaglutide dose sounds right for you, the <a href="https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=ee06186f-2aa3-4990-a760-757579d8f77b" target="_blank" rel="noopener">labeled 1.7 mg maintenance option</a> is the place to start that conversation with your prescriber.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "What do trials show about lower doses of tirzepatide?",
    body: `<p>In SURMOUNT-1, the lowest maintenance dose of tirzepatide (Zepbound 5 mg) produced 15.0% average weight loss at 72 weeks, versus 19.5% on 10 mg, 20.9% on 15 mg and 3.1% on placebo, under the treatment-regimen estimand (<a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2206038" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jastreboff et al., NEJM 2022</a>). So 5 mg is a real, effective dose, but it is a labeled maintenance dose, not a microdose.</p>
<p>SURMOUNT-1 enrolled <a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2206038" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2,539 adults with obesity or overweight and without diabetes</a>. Everyone started at 2.5 mg and stepped up to their assigned dose. No arm stayed on 2.5 mg, and no arm used anything smaller.</p>
<h3>SURMOUNT-1 by dose (72 weeks)</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>5 mg</th><th>10 mg</th><th>15 mg</th><th>Placebo</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Weight change, treatment-regimen estimand (everyone randomized)</td><td>-15.0%</td><td>-19.5%</td><td>-20.9%</td><td>-3.1%</td><td><a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2206038" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NEJM 2022</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight change, efficacy estimand (on treatment)</td><td>-16.0%</td><td>-21.4%</td><td>-22.5%</td><td>Not given in our source</td><td><a href="https://investor.lilly.com/news-releases/news-release-details/lillys-tirzepatide-delivered-225-weight-loss-adults-obesity-or" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lilly, 2022</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Lost 20% or more of body weight</td><td>Not given in our source</td><td>50%</td><td>57%</td><td>3%</td><td><a href="https://www.healio.com/news/endocrinology/20220604/weekly-tirzepatide-confers-substantial-weight-loss-in-obesity-without-diabetes-surmount1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Healio</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Nausea</td><td>24.6%</td><td>33.3%</td><td>31.0%</td><td>9.5%</td><td><a href="https://investor.lilly.com/node/47141" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lilly</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Stopped because of adverse events</td><td>4.3%</td><td>7.1%</td><td>6.2%</td><td>2.6%</td><td><a href="https://investor.lilly.com/node/47141" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lilly</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Two things stand out in the <a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2206038" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SURMOUNT-1 data</a>. First, the step from 5 mg to 10 mg added about 4.5 points of average weight loss under the treatment-regimen estimand, while the step from 10 mg to 15 mg added about 1.4 points. Second, side effects did not keep rising in a straight line: nausea and adverse-event dropouts were lowest at 5 mg but slightly higher at 10 mg than at 15 mg (<a href="https://investor.lilly.com/node/47141" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lilly</a>). Averages hide wide individual differences.</p>
<h3>What the Zepbound label says about lower doses</h3>
<p>The Zepbound label is explicit on two points, as summarized from the <a href="https://uspl.lilly.com/zepbound/zepbound.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">US prescribing information</a> and <a href="https://prescriberpoint.com/therapies/zepbound-487cd7e" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PrescriberPoint</a>:</p>
<ul>
<li>The 2.5 mg dose is for starting treatment and is not a maintenance dose.</li>
<li>Maintenance doses for weight reduction are 5, 10 or 15 mg weekly, chosen by response and tolerability, and "if patients do not tolerate a maintenance dosage, a lower maintenance dosage should be considered." For obstructive sleep apnea, the maintenance dose is 10 or 15 mg.</li>
</ul>
<p>In other words, a lower dose of tirzepatide is already built into the <a href="https://uspl.lilly.com/zepbound/zepbound.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zepbound label</a>, with a floor of 5 mg for weight maintenance. Staying on 2.5 mg long term, or going below it, is outside what the label and the trials cover.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for you:</strong> if you are on 10 or 15 mg and struggling with side effects, asking your prescriber about the 5 mg maintenance dose is a labeled, studied option. Expect somewhat less weight loss on average. If you are weighing tirzepatide against semaglutide more broadly, our <a href="/articles/tirzepatide-vs-semaglutide">tirzepatide vs semaglutide comparison</a> lays out the head-to-head data.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Can a lower dose keep weight off after you lose it?",
    body: `<p>Yes, for one labeled dose: in the SURMOUNT-MAINTAIN trial, people who stepped down from 10 or 15 mg of tirzepatide (Zepbound) to 5 mg kept more weight off than people switched to placebo, but less than people who stayed on their higher dose (<a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(26)00656-2/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horn et al., Lancet, May 2026</a>). A separate trial, ATTAIN-MAINTAIN, found that switching from Wegovy or Zepbound to the Foundayo pill kept most prior weight loss.</p>
<h3>How SURMOUNT-MAINTAIN worked</h3>
<p><a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(26)00656-2/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SURMOUNT-MAINTAIN</a> enrolled 441 US adults in a 60-week open-label tirzepatide lead-in. At week 60, 378 people who had lost at least 5% of their weight and tolerated at least 10 mg were randomized 3:3:2 for 52 more weeks: 140 continued their maximum tolerated dose (MTD, 10 or 15 mg), 144 stepped down to 5 mg and 94 switched to placebo. The results were published online on May 12, 2026 and presented at ECO 2026.</p>
<h3>Week-112 results: higher dose vs 5 mg vs placebo</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Stayed on MTD (10 or 15 mg)</th><th>Stepped down to 5 mg</th><th>Switched to placebo</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Weight change from start to week 112, as reported in The Lancet</td><td>-21.9%</td><td>-16.6%</td><td>-9.9%</td><td><a href="https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2026/05/18/18/10/surmount-maintain" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lancet via ACC</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Difference vs placebo (Lancet)</td><td>-12.0 points</td><td>-6.6 points</td><td>Reference</td><td><a href="https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2026/05/18/18/10/surmount-maintain" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ACC</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight change to week 112, efficacy estimand (ECO 2026)</td><td>-22.4%</td><td>-17.0%</td><td>-10.1%</td><td><a href="https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/can-lower-dose-tirzepatide-keep-weight-2026a1000fso" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Medscape</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Needed rescue tirzepatide (regained more than half of lost weight)</td><td>8% (11/138)</td><td>25% (35/142)</td><td>67% (60/90)</td><td><a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(26)00656-2/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lancet 2026</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Stopped because of adverse events (maintenance period)</td><td>0%</td><td>0.7%</td><td>0%</td><td><a href="https://investor.lilly.com/news-releases/news-release-details/lillys-foundayo-and-lower-dose-zepbound-helped-people-maintain" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lilly, May 2026</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Lilly said people who stayed on their maximum dose preserved all of their prior weight loss, while those who stepped down to 5 mg kept <a href="https://investor.lilly.com/news-releases/news-release-details/lillys-foundayo-and-lower-dose-zepbound-helped-people-maintain" target="_blank" rel="noopener">"all but 5.6 kg"</a> on average under the efficacy estimand. One analysis described 5 mg as preserving about 70% of the higher-dose effect (<a href="https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/can-lower-dose-tirzepatide-keep-weight-2026a1000fso" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Medscape</a>). Lancet commentator André Scheen called 5 mg a "valuable alternative to discontinuation," and the authors noted that individual response "might vary," per <a href="https://www.healio.com/news/endocrinology/20260514/switching-to-lower-zepbound-dose-better-for-maintaining-weight-loss-than-stopping-therapy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Healio (May 14, 2026)</a>.</p>
<h3>Three limits before you apply this to yourself</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rescue dosing.</strong> From week 84, anyone who regained more than half their lost weight could get rescue tirzepatide, and a quarter of the 5 mg group did (<a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(26)00656-2/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lancet 2026</a>). Part of the 5 mg result therefore reflects some people going back up.</li>
<li><strong>Who was included.</strong> In <a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(26)00656-2/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SURMOUNT-MAINTAIN</a>, everyone had already lost at least 5% and tolerated at least 10 mg for many months. The trial says nothing about starting low or never titrating up.</li>
<li><strong>What was given.</strong> Five mg is an <a href="https://prescriberpoint.com/therapies/zepbound-487cd7e" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA-labeled maintenance dose</a> delivered by branded pen or vial. SURMOUNT-MAINTAIN is not evidence for sub-labeled doses, compounded "units" or stretched intervals.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Switching to a pill: ATTAIN-MAINTAIN</h3>
<p>Foundayo (orforglipron), a once-daily GLP-1 pill, was <a href="https://investor.lilly.com/news-releases/news-release-details/fda-approves-lillys-foundayotm-orforglipron-only-glp-1-pill" target="_blank" rel="noopener">approved by FDA on April 1, 2026</a>. In the 52-week ATTAIN-MAINTAIN trial, people switched from Wegovy or Zepbound to Foundayo or placebo. Those switching from Wegovy maintained 82.4% of their prior loss and those switching from Zepbound maintained 78.0%; in kilograms, the Wegovy group kept all but 0.9 kg and the Zepbound group all but 5.0 kg (<a href="https://investor.lilly.com/news-releases/news-release-details/lillys-orforglipron-helped-people-maintain-weight-loss-after" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lilly, May 2026</a>; <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-026-04386-7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nature Medicine</a>). Foundayo is a different drug at its own labeled doses, not a smaller dose of the injection. Our <a href="/articles/foundayo-vs-wegovy-pill">Foundayo vs Wegovy pill comparison</a> covers how the two pills differ.</p>
<h3>Compared with stopping</h3>
<p>Stopping entirely leads to substantial regain in trials. In SURMOUNT-4, people switched to placebo after a 36-week tirzepatide lead-in regained 14.0% from week 36 to 88, while those who continued lost a further 5.5% (treatment-regimen estimand) (<a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12559773/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JAMA 2024</a>). A <a href="https://www.ox.ac.uk/news/2026-01-08-new-study-finds-stopping-weight-loss-drugs-linked-faster-regain-ending-diet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BMJ meta-analysis published January 7, 2026</a> found weight rose by an average of 0.4 kg a month after weight-management drugs were stopped. Our guide to <a href="/articles/stopping-glp1-medication-what-happens">what happens when you stop a GLP-1</a> goes deeper. A labeled lower dose chosen with your prescriber has trial support as a middle path; a self-chosen fraction does not.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Is the starting dose of Ozempic or Wegovy a microdose?",
    body: `<p>No. The 0.25 mg starting dose of Ozempic or Wegovy (semaglutide) is the first step of a planned dose escalation, not a microdose and not a maintenance dose. The labels use it for 4 weeks to let your body adjust before the dose goes up, and the same is true of the 2.5 mg starting dose of Zepbound or Mounjaro (tirzepatide), which the <a href="https://uspl.lilly.com/zepbound/zepbound.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zepbound label</a> says is for treatment initiation and is not a maintenance dose.</p>
<p>The Ozempic dosing page says the 0.25 mg dose for the first 4 weeks is meant to "help give your body a chance to get used to the medicine" before moving to 0.5 mg (<a href="https://www.ozempic.com/ozempic-pen/dosing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ozempic.com</a>). Ozempic is approved for type 2 diabetes; Wegovy is the semaglutide product approved for weight management.</p>
<h3>Labeled starting doses and what comes after them</h3>
<p>The table below describes what each label sets out, so you can read your own prescription. It is not a dosing plan: your prescriber decides your doses and timing.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Labeled starting dose</th><th>What the label uses it for</th><th>Labeled maintenance dose(s) for weight</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wegovy injection (semaglutide)</td><td>0.25 mg weekly, weeks 1-4</td><td>First step; then 0.5 mg, 1 mg and 1.7 mg at 4-week steps</td><td>2.4 mg (recommended) or 1.7 mg</td><td><a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/215256s024lbl.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wegovy label</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Wegovy pill (oral semaglutide)</td><td>1.5 mg daily, days 1-30</td><td>First step; then 4 mg and 9 mg</td><td>25 mg daily from day 91</td><td><a href="https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=ee06186f-2aa3-4990-a760-757579d8f77b" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DailyMed</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Ozempic (semaglutide, type 2 diabetes)</td><td>0.25 mg weekly for 4 weeks</td><td>Getting used to the medicine; then 0.5 mg</td><td>Not approved for weight loss</td><td><a href="https://www.ozempic.com/ozempic-pen/dosing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ozempic.com</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Zepbound (tirzepatide)</td><td>2.5 mg weekly</td><td>Treatment initiation only, not maintenance</td><td>5, 10 or 15 mg</td><td><a href="https://prescriberpoint.com/therapies/zepbound-487cd7e" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zepbound label summary</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Why the escalation exists</h3>
<p>Dose escalation is the label's built-in tool for tolerability. The Wegovy label says that if a dose is not tolerated during escalation, the prescriber can consider <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/215256s024lbl.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">delaying the next increase by 4 weeks</a>. That is a supervised slowdown, decided by a clinician, using labeled doses.</p>
<p>Staying on a starter dose indefinitely is different. No trial in our sources (for example <a href="https://www.ajmc.com/view/semaglutide-produces-significant-sustained-weight-loss-for-adults-with-obesity-in-phase-2-trial" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the semaglutide phase 2 trial</a> or <a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2206038" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SURMOUNT-1</a>) reports long-term weight results for people kept on 0.25 mg of semaglutide, 1.5 mg of the Wegovy pill or 2.5 mg of tirzepatide. Even the lowest phase 2 semaglutide arm added up to more than 0.25 mg a week, and every SURMOUNT-1 participant moved past 2.5 mg.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for you:</strong> if you feel well and are losing weight on a starter dose, that is useful information to share with your prescriber, not a reason to stop escalating on your own. Ask what the label says about your next step, and whether delaying it or choosing a lower labeled maintenance dose makes sense for you.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Does microdosing reduce side effects?",
    body: `<p>Lower labeled doses of GLP-1s such as tirzepatide and semaglutide do cause somewhat less nausea and fewer side-effect dropouts in trials, so the idea behind microdosing for tolerability is plausible. But no trial has measured side effects at microdoses, and the benefit seen between labeled doses, for example in the <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/spl/data/dcabc03d-6a72-467a-8043-9eb12ce9c856/dcabc03d-6a72-467a-8043-9eb12ce9c856.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zepbound label</a>, is modest, not dramatic.</p>
<p>Side effects are the main reason people say they microdose: 40.8% of microdosers in the <a href="https://www.ispor.org/heor-resources/presentations-database/presentation-cti/ispor-2026/poster-session-4-3/emerging-patterns-of-glp-1-microdosing-in-a-large-real-world-population" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Evidation ISPOR 2026 survey</a> cited side-effect management (self-reported). Here is what labels and trials show about dose and gut side effects.</p>
<h3>Gastrointestinal side effects by labeled dose</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Drug and data</th><th>Lower dose</th><th>Higher dose(s)</th><th>Placebo</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Zepbound, nausea (pooled SURMOUNT-1 and -2, label)</td><td>25% (5 mg)</td><td>29% (10 mg), 28% (15 mg)</td><td>8%</td><td><a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/spl/data/dcabc03d-6a72-467a-8043-9eb12ce9c856/dcabc03d-6a72-467a-8043-9eb12ce9c856.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zepbound label</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Zepbound, stopped for GI adverse reactions (label)</td><td>1.9% (5 mg)</td><td>3.3% (10 mg), 4.3% (15 mg)</td><td>0.5%</td><td><a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/spl/data/dcabc03d-6a72-467a-8043-9eb12ce9c856/dcabc03d-6a72-467a-8043-9eb12ce9c856.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zepbound label</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Semaglutide, any adverse event (STEP 2, type 2 diabetes)</td><td>81.8% (1.0 mg)</td><td>87.6% (2.4 mg)</td><td>Not given in our source</td><td><a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(21)00213-0/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lancet 2021</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Wegovy, nausea (adult trials, label)</td><td>Not reported by dose</td><td>44% (Wegovy)</td><td>16%</td><td><a href="https://www.novo-pi.com/wegovy.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wegovy label</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Semaglutide 2.4 mg, stopped for GI events (STEP 1)</td><td>No lower-dose arm</td><td>4.5%</td><td>0.8%</td><td><a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2032183" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NEJM 2021</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Liraglutide, GI disorders (SCALE Diabetes)</td><td>Less frequent (1.8 mg)</td><td>More frequent (3.0 mg)</td><td>Less frequent</td><td><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26284720/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JAMA 2015</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>What that data does and does not imply</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>The gap is modest.</strong> On Zepbound, nausea was 25% at 5 mg versus 28% to 29% at the higher doses, per the <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/spl/data/dcabc03d-6a72-467a-8043-9eb12ce9c856/dcabc03d-6a72-467a-8043-9eb12ce9c856.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">label</a>. A lower labeled dose still carried roughly three times the nausea rate of placebo.</li>
<li><strong>Nausea is not what causes the weight loss.</strong> A pooled analysis of SURMOUNT-1 to -4 found GI adverse events were "minimally related" to the weight reduction achieved with tirzepatide (<a href="https://dom-pubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/dom.16176" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rubino et al., Diabetes Obes Metab 2025</a>). Feeling less sick on a tiny dose does not prove the dose is working.</li>
<li><strong>Rare serious risks may not shrink with dose.</strong> One clinician quoted by <a href="https://health.yahoo.com/your-body/weight-management/weight-loss/article/microdosing-ozempic-110700989.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yahoo Health</a> argues that "GLP-1 risks are generally not dose dependent," referring to problems such as pancreatitis and gallbladder disease. That is an opinion, not a trial finding, but no trial shows those risks disappear at microdoses either. <a href="https://www.cedars-sinai.org/stories-and-insights/expert-advice/can-microdosing-glp-1s-promote-health-and-weight-loss" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cedars-Sinai</a> lists gallbladder disease, pancreatitis, thyroid conditions, retinopathy and nutrient deficiencies among the risks of unsupervised GLP-1 use.</li>
<li><strong>Self-adjusted dosing adds its own risks.</strong> FDA has received reports of overdoses, some needing hospital care, linked to confusion between milligrams, milliliters and "units" with compounded semaglutide (<a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/fda-alerts-health-care-providers-compounders-and-patients-dosing-errors-associated-compounded" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA, July 2024</a>). A plan meant to reduce side effects can cause severe ones if the measurement goes wrong.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What to do instead:</strong> the labels already give your prescriber tools for side effects, including slower escalation and lower maintenance doses. For a fuller list of side effects and warning signs, read our <a href="/articles/glp1-side-effects-risks-2026">GLP-1 side effects and risks guide</a>.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "What about microdosing for longevity, inflammation or 'food noise'?",
    body: `<p>No randomized trial in humans has reported results showing that microdosed semaglutide, tirzepatide or any GLP-1 slows aging, lowers inflammation, extends life or quiets "food noise," as of November 2, 2026. Companies sell low-dose GLP-1s for these goals, but <a href="https://www.sciencenews.org/article/glp1-medications-microdose-longevity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Science News</a> reported that "there's scant published research on GLP-1 microdosing."</p>
<h3>Longevity and aging</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>One registered human trial, no results.</strong> AgelessRx sponsors <a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07092605" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07092605</a>, described on the registry as a blinded, randomized, placebo-controlled trial of microdosed semaglutide on health, quality-of-life and longevity measures in healthy adults. Its registry status is unclear and no results have been posted (Section 11 has details).</li>
<li><strong>Mouse data.</strong> A mouse study published in Nature reported that starting semaglutide in old age extended lifespan by about 12% (<a href="https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/glp-1-weight-loss-medication-could-slow-aging-and-extend-lifespan/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scientific American, 2026</a>). Mouse results often fail to carry over to people, and the study says nothing about human microdoses.</li>
<li><strong>Standard-dose epigenetic data.</strong> A post hoc analysis of a 32-week trial in people with HIV-associated fat changes reported slower "epigenetic aging" on semaglutide (<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40791720/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed 40791720</a>). Participants were titrated to the standard 1.0 mg weekly dose, not a microdose, and aging was not the trial's main goal, so it is not evidence for microdosing.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Inflammation and heart health</h3>
<p>The proven cardiovascular benefit of semaglutide comes from the SELECT trial, which used <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/215256s024lbl.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly</a>, the full Wegovy maintenance dose. No outcome trial supports a heart or anti-inflammatory benefit from microdoses. When the STEP 1 extension stopped full-dose semaglutide, most cardiometabolic improvements drifted back toward baseline by week 120 (<a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9542252/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diabetes Obes Metab 2022</a>), which suggests the benefits depend on ongoing, effective treatment rather than a small background dose.</p>
<h3>"Food noise"</h3>
<p>Many people describe GLP-1s as quieting constant thoughts about food. That experience is common in personal accounts, but no trial in our sources has measured "food noise" at microdoses, and no human randomized trial of microdosing has reported any results at all. A low dose that seems to help your cravings is worth mentioning to your prescriber, but it is not proof of an effect.</p>
<h3>Who these programs are aimed at</h3>
<p>Some longevity-focused programs are marketed to people who would not qualify for a GLP-1 under its <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/215256s024lbl.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA-labeled weight indication</a>. If you do not have obesity or a weight-related condition, you would be taking a prescription drug with known risks, often in compounded form, for benefits no human trial has shown. <a href="https://www.statnews.com/2025/11/04/microdosing-glp-1-drugs-no-clinical-evidence-effective/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yale's Reshma Ramachandran told STAT</a> that patients in these programs are "guinea pigs, both on the efficacy side and the safety side."</p>
<p><strong>What this means for you:</strong> treat longevity and anti-inflammatory microdosing claims as unproven. If results from a properly run human trial appear, they will be news, and we will update this guide.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Which clinical trials are testing GLP-1 microdosing?",
    body: `<p>As of November 2, 2026, two registered studies on ClinicalTrials.gov are tied to microdosing semaglutide or tirzepatide: NCT07092605, a planned randomized trial sponsored by AgelessRx whose status is unclear, and NCT07588984, an observational cohort study, not a randomized trial. Neither has posted results, and both are sponsored by companies that sell GLP-1 programs (<a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07092605" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07092605</a>; <a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07588984" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07588984</a>).</p>
<p>No human randomized trial of GLP-1 microdosing has reported results. Two further registered trials (<a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07783854" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07783854</a> and <a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07859176" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07859176</a>) study tapering semaglutide doses, either as a maintenance regimen or as a way of stopping, which is related to but not the same as microdosing.</p>
<h3>Registered studies relevant to low-dose GLP-1 use (as of November 2, 2026)</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Registry ID</th><th>What it studies</th><th>Design</th><th>Sponsor</th><th>Status and results</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07092605" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07092605</a></td><td>Microdosed semaglutide (sublingual and injected) vs placebo on health, quality of life and longevity measures in healthy adults 18-65, for up to about 6 months</td><td>Described as blinded, randomized, placebo-controlled, decentralized by telemedicine</td><td>AgelessRx (sells low-dose GLP-1s)</td><td>Registry status unclear; no results posted</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07588984" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07588984</a></td><td>FLOURISH and THRIVE: GLP-1 medication (semaglutide, tirzepatide) plus behavioral programs, weight and metabolic outcomes</td><td>Observational prospective cohort, not randomized</td><td>A digital weight-loss company that sells GLP-1 programs</td><td>No results posted</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07783854" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07783854</a></td><td>Semaglutide dose-tapering regimen for keeping weight off</td><td>Open-label, non-inferiority randomized trial</td><td>Union Hospital, Tongji Medical College (Wuhan, China)</td><td>Not yet recruiting; estimated start Sept 1, 2026; primary completion Dec 30, 2027</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07859176" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07859176</a></td><td>Tapered vs abrupt stopping of semaglutide after treating recurrent weight gain or suboptimal response following bariatric surgery</td><td>Comparison of tapered and abrupt discontinuation</td><td>Not retrieved</td><td>Status not retrieved; no results found</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Why the AgelessRx trial's status is unclear</h3>
<p>Different sources describe <a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07092605" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07092605</a> differently. A registry mirror lists it as "Early Phase 1" with an estimated 150 participants and "invitation-only" status (<a href="https://ctv.veeva.com/study/effectiveness-of-microdosed-glp-1-in-improving-health-quality-of-life-and-longevity-measures" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Veeva CTV</a>), while other reports conflict on whether it has enrolled at all. We could not confirm its current status, so check the ClinicalTrials.gov record yourself before relying on any claim that it is "ongoing" or "complete." What is clear: no results have been posted. AgelessRx refers to it as ALRx010 and describes it as randomized and placebo-controlled (<a href="https://agelessrx.com/glp1-microdosing-trial/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AgelessRx</a>).</p>
<h3>Why the second study cannot settle the question</h3>
<p><a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07588984" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCT07588984</a> follows people who are already in a company's GLP-1 programs. Without randomization, people who choose one program may differ from people who choose another in ways that affect weight, so an observational cohort can describe outcomes but cannot prove that a lower dose caused them. The registry also lists individual participant data sharing as "No."</p>
<p><strong>How to read future results:</strong> look for randomized, placebo-controlled designs, peer-reviewed publication rather than a press release, and the exact product tested. Results for one company's sublingual semaglutide would not automatically apply to another pharmacy's compounded injection.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "What do doctors and medical groups say about microdosing?",
    body: `<p>Obesity specialists and medical groups that have spoken publicly do not support microdosing GLP-1s such as semaglutide or tirzepatide outside of supervised, labeled dosing. The president of the American Association of Clinical Endocrinology (AACE) said in October 2025 that AACE "does not support the routine use of microdosing GLP-1RA medications in clinical care" (<a href="https://www.aace.com/in-the-news/glp-1ra-microdosing-trend-continues-despite-anecdotal-evidence" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AACE</a>), and an Obesity Medicine Association working group found "little to no peer-reviewed evidence" for it (<a href="https://www.sochob.cl/web1/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Microdosing-of-obesity-medications-A-Perspective-Statement-from-the-Access-and-Policy-Working-Group.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Obesity Pillars, 2026</a>).</p>
<p>Knowing what kind of statement each view is helps, because an interview quote, a working-group perspective and a formal guideline carry different weight.</p>
<h3>Positions on GLP-1 microdosing (as of November 2026)</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>What they said</th><th>Type of statement</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>AACE (Scott D. Isaacs, MD, president)</td><td>Does "not support the routine use of microdosing"; recommends adherence to FDA-approved dosing</td><td>Interview quote from AACE's president, Oct 2025; not a formal position paper</td><td><a href="https://www.pharmaceutical-technology.com/news/glp-1ra-microdosing-trend-continues-despite-anecdotal-evidence/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pharmaceutical Technology</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Obesity Medicine Association, Access and Policy Working Group</td><td>"Little to no peer-reviewed evidence" for the safety or effectiveness of microdosed regimens; concerns about safety, efficacy, oversight and non-medical use</td><td>Working-group perspective in Obesity Pillars (2026)</td><td><a href="https://www.sochob.cl/web1/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Microdosing-of-obesity-medications-A-Perspective-Statement-from-the-Access-and-Policy-Working-Group.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Obesity Pillars</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Jody Dushay, MD (Beth Israel Deaconess, Harvard Medical School)</td><td>"Microdosing GLP-1s is not a thing"; no legitimate long-term data support it</td><td>Op-ed, STAT First Opinion, May 29, 2026</td><td><a href="https://www.statnews.com/2026/05/29/glp-1-microdose-popular-but-unsupported-by-evidence/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">STAT</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Amanda Velazquez, MD (Cedars-Sinai)</td><td>No evidence low-dose, intermittent use gives meaningful or sustained weight loss; avoid unsupervised use at any dose</td><td>Health-system expert article, 2026</td><td><a href="https://www.cedars-sinai.org/stories-and-insights/expert-advice/can-microdosing-glp-1s-promote-health-and-weight-loss" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cedars-Sinai</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Judith Korner, MD (NewYork-Presbyterian)</td><td>Taking GLP-1s at self-chosen doses "on your own is not safe"</td><td>Health-system article</td><td><a href="https://healthmatters.nyp.org/what-is-glp-1-microdosing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NewYork-Presbyterian</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Reshma Ramachandran, MD (Yale)</td><td>Patients in microdose programs are "guinea pigs"</td><td>News interview, Nov 2025</td><td><a href="https://www.statnews.com/2025/11/04/microdosing-glp-1-drugs-no-clinical-evidence-effective/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">STAT</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Kiran Khanani, PharmD (Optum)</td><td>Lower dosing "can be safe" if done on a doctor's recommendation and under supervision</td><td>Pharmacist blog; the moderate end of opinion</td><td><a href="https://perks.optum.com/blog/glp-1-microdosing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Optum Perks</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Hackensack Meridian Health endocrinologist Ritu Anand, MD, DABOM, warns that the effects of microdosing "have not been clinically tested" and that these doses are not FDA approved, and the health system's summary of survey data says nearly half of microdosers had not consulted a doctor before changing their dose (<a href="https://www.hackensackmeridianhealth.org/en/healthier-you/2026/05/12/is-microdosing-glp-1s-safe" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hackensack Meridian Health, May 12, 2026</a>).</p>
<h3>Groups with no formal statement</h3>
<p>We found no formal public statement on microdosing from The Obesity Society, the American Society for Metabolic and Bariatric Surgery (ASMBS), the Endocrine Society or the American Diabetes Association as of November 2026. Existing joint guidance from The Obesity Society and partner groups addresses <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12997229/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">labeled dosing only</a>. Silence is not endorsement; it means these groups have not weighed in formally. We also found no Cleveland Clinic or Mayo Clinic page specifically on GLP-1 microdosing, so we do not attribute a position to either.</p>
<h3>Where doctors differ</h3>
<p>Clinicians agree that microdosing as marketed is untested and that self-directed dose changes are risky. The difference is tone. Some clinicians reject the idea outright; others, such as the <a href="https://perks.optum.com/blog/glp-1-microdosing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Optum pharmacist</a>, accept that a lower dose can be appropriate for some people when a doctor recommends and monitors it. On stepping down to a labeled dose, Lancet commentator André Scheen described a 5 mg tirzepatide maintenance dose as a "valuable alternative to discontinuation" (<a href="https://www.healio.com/news/endocrinology/20260514/switching-to-lower-zepbound-dose-better-for-maintaining-weight-loss-than-stopping-therapy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Healio, May 2026</a>).</p>
<p><strong>What this means for you:</strong> if your prescriber suggests a lower labeled dose, that is in line with expert opinion. If a website or influencer suggests a self-measured fraction, the experts above are close to unanimous that you should not do it on your own.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Why do some telehealth companies sell 'microdose' plans?",
    body: `<p>Telehealth companies sell "microdose" GLP-1 plans because there is demand from people worried about side effects and cost, and because compounded semaglutide and tirzepatide can be made in amounts that FDA-approved pens do not offer. These plans almost always use compounded drugs, which are not FDA-approved and are not generic versions of Wegovy, Ozempic or Zepbound, and no trial has shown that the low-dose versions work.</p>
<h3>How these plans came about</h3>
<p>In November 2025, <a href="https://www.statnews.com/2025/11/04/microdosing-glp-1-drugs-no-clinical-evidence-effective/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">STAT reported</a> that several telehealth companies, including Found and Hims &amp; Hers, had launched "microdosed" GLP-1 programs in the previous three months, under the headline that microdosing aims to extend the life of the GLP-1 compounding market. The Obesity Medicine Association working group notes that fractional doses are "not available in FDA-approved pens," so they usually depend on compounded products (<a href="https://www.sochob.cl/web1/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Microdosing-of-obesity-medications-A-Perspective-Statement-from-the-Access-and-Policy-Working-Group.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Obesity Pillars, 2026</a>). Commentators describe 503A pharmacies, which compound for individual patients with a documented clinical need, as the legal route "personalized" low-dose offerings rely on; that is a legal interpretation, not FDA wording.</p>
<h3>Regulators are watching this market</h3>
<ul>
<li>On February 6, 2026, FDA said it intends to take "decisive steps" to restrict GLP-1 ingredients used in mass-marketed compounded drugs (<a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/02/06/fda-to-restrict-glp-1-ingredients-used-in-nonapproved-compounded-drugs.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNBC</a>).</li>
<li>On March 3, 2026, FDA announced 30 warning letters to telehealth companies over false or misleading claims about compounded GLP-1s, such as implying they are equivalent to approved drugs (<a href="https://www.patientcareonline.com/view/fda-issues-warning-letters-30-telehealth-companies-over-misleading-compounded-glp-1-ra-marketing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Patient Care Online</a>). About 25 more followed in June 2026 (<a href="https://www.sheppard.com/insights/blogs/fdas-focus-returns-to-compounding-and-telehealth-another-wave-of-warning-letters" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sheppard Mullin</a>).</li>
<li>On April 30, 2026, FDA proposed keeping semaglutide, tirzepatide and liraglutide off the 503B bulks list, which would bar large-scale compounding by outsourcing facilities; the proposal was not final as of late October 2026 (<a href="https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/fda-proposes-end-mass-glp-1-compounding-2026a1000e06" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Medscape</a>).</li>
<li>As of May 31, 2026, FDA had received 990 adverse event reports tied to compounded semaglutide and more than 730 tied to compounded tirzepatide, and says the true numbers are likely higher (<a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-alerts-and-statements/fdas-concerns-unapproved-glp-1-drugs-used-weight-loss" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA</a>). In September 2026, a pharmacy recalled 22 lots of compounded tirzepatide for "lack of assurance of sterility" (<a href="https://www.bostonglobe.com/2026/10/21/business/glp-1-alternative-drugs-face-nationwide-recall/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Boston Globe</a>).</li>
</ul>
<p>For the quality and regulatory differences, see <a href="/articles/compounded-semaglutide-vs-brand-name">compounded semaglutide vs brand-name</a>.</p>
<h3>Providers we review that list microdose options</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>What it lists</th><th>Published price for the microdose option</th><th>What we can and cannot say</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><a href="/reviews/bmimd">bmiMD</a></td><td>Compounded semaglutide and tirzepatide, including "microdose versions" of both</td><td>None advertised; plan pricing shows at checkout</td><td>The offering exists; we have no data on outcomes for its microdose versions</td></tr>
<tr><td>welludr</td><td>"GLP-1 plus NAD+/B12 microdose"</td><td>Not published separately in our data</td><td>No trial supports adding NAD+ or B12 to a GLP-1 microdose for weight loss</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Listing these options is not a recommendation of the microdose plans, and not evidence that they work.</p>
<h3>A lower dose may not cost you less</h3>
<p>Several compounded providers in our price index charge the same monthly price at every dose. As of our October 2026 price check, altRx lists compounded semaglutide at $89 and tirzepatide at $149 a month "flat at every dose," DirectMeds lists both at $147 "flat at every dose," and TrimRx lists semaglutide at $149 "same price at every dose" (<a href="/cheapest-glp1">cheapest GLP-1 prices</a>). On plans like these, taking less medicine does not lower your bill. Brand-name Wegovy pens are similar: NovoCare's self-pay price is <a href="https://www.novocare.com/patient/medicines/wegovy.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">$349 a month for every dose from 0.25 to 2.4 mg</a>. A later section works through the full cost math.</p>
<p><strong>Before you sign up for any microdose plan, ask:</strong> which drug and which pharmacy makes it, whether it is compounded, how the dose is written (milligrams, not just "units"), who decides dose changes, and whether a labeled option would meet the same goal. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-choose-a-glp1-provider">how to choose a GLP-1 provider</a> covers what to check.</p>`,
  },
];
